<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Ross (Cherokee chief)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1790">1790</a>–<a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Ross (1790–1866) was Principal Chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> Nation from 1828 until his death. Of mixed Scottish and Cherokee descent, he led the nation's legal and political resistance to removal, including its cases before the US Supreme Court, and opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-new-echota">Treaty of New Echota</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trail-of-tears">Trail of Tears</a>, on which his wife Quatie died, he led the reunited nation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>, including through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-ross-cherokee-chief.jpg" alt="John Ross (Cherokee chief)" width="320" height="373" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Ross_of_the_Cherokee.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ross, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> as Guwisguwi, was born on 3 October 1790 at Turkeytown on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coosa-river">Coosa River</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, to Mollie McDonald and Daniel Ross, a Scots trader. Under the Cherokee matrilineal system he belonged to his mother's family and the Bird Clan. He grew up near Lookout Mountain, was taught in English and spoke Cherokee poorly. After schooling at home he studied with the Reverend Gideon Blackburn and finished at an academy at South West Point near Kingston, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At twenty Ross was appointed US Indian agent to the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> he was adjutant of a Cherokee regiment and fought under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> at the Battle of Horseshoe Bend against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sticks">Red Sticks</a>. He grew wealthy from a tobacco plantation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> worked by about 20 slaves, and in 1816 founded Ross's Landing, a ferry and trading post later renamed Chattanooga. Trained by Principal Chief Pathkiller and Charles R. Hicks, he became president of the National Committee in November 1818 and refused an offer of $200,000 for voluntary removal west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>.</p><p>Elected principal chief on 17 October 1828, Ross found allies in Congress such as Henry Clay, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-webster">Daniel Webster</a> and Davy Crockett, but in 1829 President Jackson backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>'s claim to extend its laws over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a>. On 7 November 1835 the Georgia guard arrested Ross at his home. After the Treaty Party signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-new-echota">Treaty of New Echota</a>, General Winfield Scott and 7,000 federal troops enforced removal in 1838, and Ross persuaded Scott to let him supervise much of it. In 1844 he married Mary Stapler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>.</p><p>When the Civil War came Ross urged neutrality, but on 21 August 1861 he told a general assembly that, for the sake of tribal and inter-Indian unity, the nation should ally with the Confederacy. Stand Watie and many wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> backed the Confederacy, while the Keetoowah Society and other traditionalists stayed loyal to the Union.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ross died on 1 August 1866 and was first buried beside Mary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilmington-delaware">Wilmington, Delaware</a>; a few months later the Cherokee Nation moved his remains to the Ross Cemetery at Park Hill, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>. Several Cherokee Freedmen he had owned kept his surname, and one, Stick Ross, sat in the Cherokee Legislature from 1893 to 1894. His former home in Rossville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, is a National Historic Landmark, and his great-great-granddaughter Mary G. Ross became the first Native American woman engineer.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Ross (Cherokee chief)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Turkeytown, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.08,-85.88&amp;year=1839" aria-label="Turkeytown on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2363 929 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Ross (Cherokee chief)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2813 1199h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2813 1199h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.08,-85.88&amp;year=1839">What was here in 1839? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We are denationalized; we are disfranchised. We are deprived of membership in the human family! We have neither land nor home, nor resting place that can be called our own.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the Senate and House of Representatives against the Treaty of New Echota, 1836 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.digitalhistory.uh.edu/active_learning/explorations/indian_removal/ross3.cfm" rel="noopener">www.digitalhistory.uh.edu</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Our property may be plundered before our eyes; violence may be committed on our persons; even our lives may be taken away, and there is none to regard our complaints.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the Senate and House of Representatives against the Treaty of New Echota, 1836 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.digitalhistory.uh.edu/active_learning/explorations/indian_removal/ross3.cfm" rel="noopener">www.digitalhistory.uh.edu</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The instrument in question is not the act of our Nation; we are not parties to its covenants; it has not received the sanction of our people.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the Senate and House of Representatives against the Treaty of New Echota, 1836 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.digitalhistory.uh.edu/active_learning/explorations/indian_removal/ross3.cfm" rel="noopener">www.digitalhistory.uh.edu</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We are overwhelmed! Our hearts are sickened, our utterance is paralized, when we reflect on the condition in which we are placed…&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the Senate and House of Representatives against the Treaty of New Echota, 1836 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.digitalhistory.uh.edu/active_learning/explorations/indian_removal/ross3.cfm" rel="noopener">www.digitalhistory.uh.edu</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
